  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1225 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nHeat Advisory criteria remain more likely to occur on Wednesday,\nwith the biggest messaging change for the forecast being the\nissuance of an advisory for the highest confidence areas. Later\nshifts may need to reevaluate mainly the northern extent of the\nadvisory, depending on potential for cloud cover and isolated\nshowers and thunderstorms during the afternoon in response to a\nweak front approaching and stalling near the Kansas border. NBM\nPOPs have been increased slightly to account for the low\nthunderstorm potential during this time frame. The movement of the\nfront and where it hangs up remains in question, placing a lower\nconfidence in the forecast temperatures Wednesday night into\nThursday for areas roughly north of Highway 412. To the south of\nthis boundary, additional heat advisories may be needed for \nThursday. Low shower and thunderstorm chances will continue into\nthe afternoon Thursday. Thursday night and into Friday, a better\npotential for showers and thunderstorms, including a very low\nchance of strong to severe storms and locally heavy rain, exists\nas more northwesterly flow develops across the area on the eastern\nedge of the upper ridge in the southwestern United States and a\ndisturbances pushes through the area. The best chances remain\nlikely to be confined to areas along and north of I-40. The rain\nand increased cloud cover on Friday will drop temperatures back\ncloser to normal. This cooldown will be short lived, however, as\nthe ridge expands westward once again, leading to hot temperatures\nand dry conditions again for the weekend.",
